THOUGHT: `find(key, true)` is annotated as returning `@Nullable Node<K,V>`, so NullAway flags `created.value`. In practice, when `create` is true, `find` should always return a non-null node, but the type system doesn’t know that. The safest fix is to check for null and handle it, ideally in a way that preserves behavior. Since `put` must not return null for existing keys and should insert otherwise, we can treat a null `created` as “no previous value” and avoid dereferencing it. I’ll implement a null check and only access `created.value` when non-null, returning null otherwise.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/gson && sed -i '96,103s/Node<K, V> created = find(key, true);
    V result = created.value;
    created.value = value;
    return result;/Node<K, V> created = find(key, true);
    if (created == null) {
      return null;
    }
    V result = created.value;
    created.value = value;
    return result;/' gson/src/main/java/com/google/gson/internal/LinkedTreeMap.java
```

